    {
      "filename": "9.2inch Coastal Defence Gun – IWM Duxford (51290142981).jpg",
      "title": "File:9.2inch Coastal Defence Gun – IWM Duxford (51290142981).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/9.2inch_Coastal_Defence_Gun_%E2%80%93_IWM_Duxford_%2851290142981%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9.2inch_Coastal_Defence_Gun_%E2%80%93_IWM_Duxford_(51290142981).jpg",
      "width": 5161,
      "height": 3441,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Alan Wilson from Peterborough, Cambs, UK",
      "description": "Originally installed at the Spur Battery in Gibraltar during 1902, this gun was typical of those used across the British Empire to protect strategically important coastal sites. The Battery was 1,130ft above sea level and 250ft below the top of the Gibraltar Rock. It was used in action during the First World War when it took part in the bombardment of German Submarines spotted off the coast on 31st December 1915.\nThe barrel was replaced in 1929 and in 1935 it was converted from a Mk5 to a Mk7, with increased armour and updated control systems. It was last fired in 1973 when 29 rounds were fired at a towed target, which received several direct hits.\nReplaced by an Exocet guided missile system, it was dismantled by Royal Engineers and installed at Duxford during 1982.\nImperial War Museum\nDuxford Airfield, Cambridgeshire, UK.\n\n23rd May 2021",
      "dateCreated": "2021-05-23 11:44",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "imperial-war-museum-duxford-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u120"
    },
    {
      "filename": "A27L Centaur Command Tank ‘T187617’ – IWM Duxford (51291803222).jpg",
      "title": "File:A27L Centaur Command Tank ‘T187617’ – IWM Duxford (51291803222).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/A27L_Centaur_Command_Tank_%E2%80%98T187617%E2%80%99_%E2%80%93_IWM_Duxford_%2851291803222%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A27L_Centaur_Command_Tank_%E2%80%98T187617%E2%80%99_%E2%80%93_IWM_Duxford_(51291803222).jpg",
      "width": 5321,
      "height": 3547,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Alan Wilson from Peterborough, Cambs, UK",
      "description": "The Centaur was effectively a Cromwell with a 410hp Liberty engine in place of the Cromwell’s 600hp Rolls-Royce Meteor. It was only used in action by the Royal Marines Armoured Support Group during D-Day and was a failure as a battle-tank. They were mostly retained for driver-training in the UK but some were converted to Command Tanks and used by either tank formation commanders or artillery observation officers. The main gun was removed to make room in the turret for additional radio equipment and a fake gun was fitted outside so that it appeared identical to the armed vehicles it would accompany.\nThis example is part of the Imperial War Museum collection and without any known service history it has been painted to represent a command tank of 7th Armoured Brigade photographed in Germany during 1945. \nIt seen on display in the Land Warfare Hall at Duxford Airfield, Cambridgeshire, UK.\n\n23rd May 2021",
      "dateCreated": "2021-05-23 12:36",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "imperial-war-museum-duxford-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u120"
    },
    {
      "filename": "A41 Centurion III ’07 BA 26’ (51295950790).jpg",
      "title": "File:A41 Centurion III ’07 BA 26’ (51295950790).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/A41_Centurion_III_%E2%80%9907_BA_26%E2%80%99_%2851295950790%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A41_Centurion_III_%E2%80%9907_BA_26%E2%80%99_(51295950790).jpg",
      "width": 4639,
      "height": 3093,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Alan Wilson from Peterborough, Cambs, UK",
      "description": "British Post-war era Main Battle Tank\nOfficial Designation:- FV4007, A41, Tank Medium, Centurion Mark 3\nProduced from 1946 to 1962 with a total production of 4,423\nThe Mark 3 Centurion introduced the QF 20-pounder rifled gun in place of the QF 17-pounder. This example was in active service from July 1952 until March 1957 and was then used as a training aid by a Territorial Army unit. In 1979 the exterior was restored by 38 Central Workshop REME and it was transferred to the Imperial War Museum by the Ministry of Defence. It arrived at Duxford in 1982 and is seen on display in the Land Warfare Hall at Duxford Airfield, Cambridgeshire, UK.\n23rd May 2021\n\nNOTE:- The IWM has two Centurion 3s and it’s possible that I have combined / confused their histories. The history related above would seem to apply to an unmodified example in original Korean War condition, a description I have attached to the Centurion I photographed outside. Again, if anyone ‘in the know’ would like to update me then I would be very grateful!",
      "dateCreated": "2021-05-23 12:58",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "imperial-war-museum-duxford-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u120"
    },
